{"causes": ["The inheritance of the Lordship of Biscay from his father, Lope Díaz II de Haro, in 1236 provided him with significant power and territorial influence.", "The ongoing consolidation of the Crown of Castile under Ferdinand III and Alfonso X created inherent tensions with powerful regional magnates like the Haro family.", "The strategic importance of Biscay and its resources, including iron mines and ports, made its control a critical objective for both the Haro lords and the Castilian monarchy."], "effects": ["His sustained opposition to Alfonso X contributed to the decentralization efforts of the Castilian nobility against royal authority in the mid-13th century.", "His death in 1254 immediately triggered a succession crisis within the Lordship of Biscay, leading to further conflict between his heirs and the Castilian crown.", "The assertion of Biscayan autonomy during his rule helped define the unique legal and political status of the Lordship within the broader Castilian realm for centuries."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "lope-diaz-ii-de-haro", "sourceName": "Lope Díaz II de Haro", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "diego-lpez-iii-de-haro", "targetName": "Diego López III de Haro", "context": "Diego López III de Haro succeeded his father, Lope Díaz II de Haro, as Lord of Biscay in 1236, inheriting significant power and responsibilities."}, {"sourceSlug": "diego-lpez-iii-de-haro", "sourceName": "Diego López III de Haro", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "castile", "targetName": "Castile", "context": "Diego López III de Haro's political actions and conflicts were centrally situated within the Kingdom of Castile during the 13th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "alfonso-x-of-castile", "sourceName": "Alfonso X of Castile", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "diego-lpez-iii-de-haro", "targetName": "Diego López III de Haro", "context": "The centralizing policies and territorial demands of King Alfonso X of Castile significantly influenced Diego López III de